<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madurai Nayak dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a>–<a href="/years/1736">1736</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nayaks of Madurai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> Nayak dynasty ruled much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> from 1529 to 1736. Its founders were governors for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> who became independent after Vijayanagara's defeat in 1565, ruling from Madurai and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>. Thirumalai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> (r. 1623–1659) is remembered for his palace and for additions to the Meenakshi temple. The line ended in 1736, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanda-sahib">Chanda Sahib</a> overthrew Queen Meenakshi.</p></div>
